### FTMS API - a working request collection.
###
### Run the API first (dotnet run --project src/FTMS.Api), then send these top to bottom.
### Requests 3 onwards need the token from request 2, which is captured automatically.
###
### Works in Visual Studio, VS Code (REST Client extension) and Rider.

@host = http://localhost:5150

### Every endpoint except login, refresh and health requires a bearer token.
### Expect 401.
GET {{host}}/api/transactions
Accept: application/json

### 1. Health. The only other anonymous endpoint.
GET {{host}}/health

### 2. Sign in.
### Four accounts are seeded in Development, one per role:
###   capturer / Capturer#2026   - create and update
###   manager  / Manager#2026    - everything Capturer can do, plus delete
###   auditor  / Auditor#2026    - read only
###   admin    / Admin#2026!x    - user administration, and NO transaction rights by design
###
### The response body carries the access token. The refresh token is NOT in the body - it comes
### back as an httpOnly cookie, which is what stops a script from ever reading it.
# @name signIn
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userName": "manager",
  "password": "Manager#2026"
}

### Everything below reuses the token from the response above.
@token = {{signIn.response.body.accessToken}}

### 3. Who am I? Useful for checking which roles a token actually carries.
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 4. All transaction statuses. The brief's GET /api/transactionstatuses.
GET {{host}}/api/transactionstatuses
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: application/json

### 5. Active transactions. The brief's GET /api/transactions.
### Called bare it returns Active only, which is what the brief asks for. The query parameters
### are additive: paging, sorting and an explicit status filter.
GET {{host}}/api/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: application/json

### 5b. The same list, paged and sorted.
GET {{host}}/api/transactions?page=1&pageSize=10&sortBy=amount&sortDirection=desc
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: application/json

### 5c. Inactive transactions, to see soft deleted rows still in the database.
GET {{host}}/api/transactions?status=Inactive
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: application/json

### 6. Create. The brief's POST /api/transactions.
### The server assigns the id, sets the status to Active, and stamps CreatedAtUtc - none of them
### are inputs. currencyCode is optional and defaults to ZAR.
### Responds 201 with a Location header and an ETag.
# @name create
POST {{host}}/api/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionDate": "2026-08-20T09:30:00Z",
  "transactionType": "Deposit",
  "amount": 1500.00,
  "currencyCode": "ZAR"
}

###
@id = {{create.response.body.id}}
@etag = {{create.response.headers.ETag}}

### 7. Get one by id. The brief's GET /api/transactions/{id}.
### Returns a transaction in ANY status, including Inactive, because this endpoint is the audit
### window. Carries an ETag for the update below.
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/{{id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: application/json

### 8. Update. The brief's PUT /api/transactions/{id}.
### Only transactionDate and transactionType are modifiable - amount, currency and status are not
### on the contract at all, so they cannot even be attempted.
###
### If-Match is OPTIONAL. Without it this is last-write-wins, which is what the brief describes.
PUT {{host}}/api/transactions/{{id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionDate": "2026-08-21T10:00:00Z",
  "transactionType": "Transfer"
}

### 8b. The same update, done safely.
### Sending the ETag makes it a compare-and-swap: if somebody else changed the row since you read
### it, you get 412 instead of silently overwriting them. Real clients should do this - the
### Angular client always does.
PUT {{host}}/api/transactions/{{id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: {{etag}}

{
  "transactionDate": "2026-08-22T11:15:00Z",
  "transactionType": "Payment"
}

### 8c. A deliberately stale ETag. Expect 412 Precondition Failed, and nothing changed.
PUT {{host}}/api/transactions/{{id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: "AAAAAAAAAAE="

{
  "transactionDate": "2026-08-23T08:00:00Z",
  "transactionType": "Withdrawal"
}

### 9. Delete. The brief's DELETE /api/transactions/{id}.
### Does NOT remove the row. It moves TransactionStatusId to Inactive and leaves everything else
### in place. Requires the Manager role - a Capturer token gets 403 here.
### Idempotent: send it twice and both return 204.
DELETE {{host}}/api/transactions/{{id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 9b. Proof the row survived. Status should read "Inactive".
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/{{id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Accept: application/json

### 10. Validation. Expect 400 with a per field errors dictionary.
POST {{host}}/api/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transactionDate": "2026-08-20T09:30:00Z",
  "transactionType": "NotAType",
  "amount": -5,
  "currencyCode": "RANDS"
}

### 11. Separation of duty. Sign in as Admin, then try to read transactions.
### Expect 403: administering the system and moving money through it are different jobs.
# @name signInAdmin
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userName": "admin",
  "password": "Admin#2026!x"
}

###
GET {{host}}/api/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{signInAdmin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 12. Sign out. Revokes the refresh token so the session cannot be renewed.
### The access token stays valid for its remaining minutes - it cannot be revoked, which is
### exactly why it only lasts fifteen.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
